    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "title",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "# FeatureRanker Results for Fantasy Points and Injury Risk",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "technical-summary",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Technical summary\n\n**Prior-year games is the strongest exact feature for both targets.** The points line then emphasizes prior-year scoring production across receiving, passing, and rushing. The injury line emphasizes participation and receiving workload, followed by draft and age context. These are predictive associations within the reconstructed preseason candidate pool, not causal effects.",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Scope and target definitions\n\n**Points** is next-season ESPN full-PPR regular-season points for the pooled QB, RB, WR, and TE line. The discovery sample contains 8,613 player-seasons from 2013-2021, including explicit zero outcomes for candidates who did not participate.\n\n**Injury** is any physical designation on an official regular-season injury or practice report for QB, RB, WR, TE, and K. The discovery sample contains 9,077 player-seasons with 36.8% positives. It does not measure games missed, injury severity, or biological fragility.",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "points-finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Points ranking is led by availability and prior production\n\n`lag1_games` ranks first and lands in every component method\u2019s top ten. `lag1_receptions` ranks second with the same unanimity. Prior passing touchdowns, receiving yards, receiving first downs, chunk receptions, and passing yards fill most of the remaining top positions. Draft context enters below those recent production signals.\n\nThe chart shows the primary consensus score. It is a voting score, not an effect size, so compare ordering and method agreement rather than interpreting score ratios.",
        "layout": "full",
        "sourceId": "points-ranking"
      },
      {
        "id": "points-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "points-top-chart",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "points-table",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "points-top-table",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "injury-finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Injury ranking is dominated by exposure, not recent injury history\n\n`lag1_games` again ranks first across all five methods. Receiving yards after catch, receiving yards, receptions, and offense snaps complete the top five. Draft availability, draft number, age, rookie status, and weight also enter the top 15.\n\nThe strongest injury-history feature is two-year-lag report weeks at consensus rank 44. That makes prior injury evidence useful but secondary to whether a player is established and exposed enough to accumulate report opportunities.",
        "layout": "full",
        "sourceId": "injury-ranking"
      },
      {
        "id": "injury-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "injury-top-chart",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "injury-table",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "injury-top-table",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "methodology",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Methodology keeps the two targets separate\n\nThe executed notebook rebuilt each matrix from the project\u2019s frozen local feature code and inputs. Audit seasons were excluded from ranking. `featureranker` 3.0.4 ran Random Forest, XGBoost, mutual information, ANOVA F-test, and L1 ranking independently for regression and classification.\n\nThe primary consensus uses reciprocal-rank voting weighted by each method\u2019s cross-validated linear-probe skill. Two constant points columns and seven constant injury columns were removed. No missing or infinite values remained after preprocessing.",
        "layout": "full",
        "sourceId": "notebook"
      },
      {
        "id": "robustness",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Weight choice is stable, but rank aggregation still matters\n\nAutomatic and equal method weighting produce identical top-20 feature sets for both targets. Changing the voting geometry has a larger effect: points retains 15 of 20 under Borda and 17 under exponential voting, while injury retains 11 under either alternative.\n\nThe probe table shows why weights are similar across methods. Those probes use random three-fold splits and are diagnostics, not a substitute for the project\u2019s chronological player-aware audits.",
        "layout": "full",
        "sourceId": "robustness-summary"
      },
      {
        "id": "robustness-evidence",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "robustness-table",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "probe-evidence",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "probe-table",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "limitations",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Limitations and uncertainty\n\n- Correlated variants can divide or duplicate importance. Exact engineered-feature order should not be mistaken for a unique causal mechanism.\n- Both candidate definitions include nonparticipants as zero or negative outcomes, so participation and league persistence are part of the signal.\n- The injury label captures report exposure, not medical severity. High-usage players have more opportunities to appear on a report.\n- FeatureRanker\u2019s internal cross-validation is random rather than chronological or grouped by player. Audit labels were withheld from ranking, but no new blinded prospective outcome is available yet.\n- Kickers are excluded from the primary points line because the project models them separately and labels that projection low confidence.",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "next-steps",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Recommended next steps\n\n1. Keep `lag1_games` as a must-have feature and preserve prior-year receptions, receiving yards, passing touchdowns, and passing yards in the points line.\n2. Keep participation, snaps, receiving workload, draft context, age, and the available two-year injury history in the injury line.\n3. Evaluate feature families, not only exact columns, with grouped permutation importance on chronological folds to reduce correlated-variant instability.\n4. Do not replace either production model with a small top-N list until a chronological rerun demonstrates that accuracy remains within the project\u2019s declared tolerance.",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "further-questions",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Further questions\n\n- Does grouped ranking by football concept, such as availability, receiving volume, or draft capital, remain stable across positions?\n- How much of injury discrimination survives among players with at least eight prior-year games?\n- Would a player-grouped chronological probe materially reorder the injury top 20?\n- Can a compact family-level subset match the full model on the untouched 2026 prospective outcomes?",
        "layout": "full"
      }
    ]
  },
